'''Sensory processing''' is the transformation and organization of signals from sensory receptors and neural pathways. It allows organisms to respond to features such as light, sound, touch, body position and chemicals.

== Transduction and coding ==
Sensory receptors respond to particular forms of physical or chemical stimulation. Mechanoreceptors respond to deformation, photoreceptors to light and chemoreceptors to chemical conditions. Transduction mechanisms convert stimulation into graded cellular responses or downstream spike activity. Receptive fields describe regions or stimulus dimensions influencing a neuron's response. Adaptation can alter sensitivity over time; coding depends on the sensory modality, stimulus and circuit.

== Computational and physiological analysis ==
A simple tuning model expresses firing rate r as a function of stimulus x and context c. Measure repeated trials to estimate response distributions rather than equate a single spike count with the stimulus. Signal detection theory distinguishes sensitivity from decision criterion: under equal-variance Gaussian assumptions <math>d'=\Phi^{-1}(H)-\Phi^{-1}(F)</math> for hit rate H and false-alarm rate F. These behavioral estimates do not identify the specific neural circuit without additional evidence.

== Integration and limitations ==
Neural responses may depend on prior context, attention and signals from other modalities. Psychophysics, electrophysiology and imaging provide complementary measurements with different resolution and confounds. Distinguish neural transduction, conscious perception and clinically assessed sensory difficulties; broad behavioral labels are not diagnoses. In engineered systems, sensors and signal processors can perform analogous transformations without implying that they experience sensations.

